# Provider Aliases in Terraform: Root Module to Child Modules

## How Provider Aliases Work

In Terraform, you can create multiple configurations of the same provider using the `alias` argument. As stated in the documentation, "For alternate configurations, the reference uses a `<PROVIDER>.<ALIAS>` format."

### Root Module Setup

In your root module, define multiple provider configurations with different aliases:

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-1"
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "usw2"
  region = "us-west-2"
}
```

The first configuration (without an alias) becomes the default. The second configuration with `alias = "usw2"` is an alternate configuration you can reference as `aws.usw2`.

## Passing Aliased Providers to Child Modules

The `providers` meta-argument enables passing specific provider configurations from parent to child modules. According to the documentation, this argument "instructs Terraform to use the reference provider configuration to create the module resources."

### Basic Example

```hcl
module "example" {
  source    = "./example"
  providers = {
    aws = aws.usw2
  }
}
```

Here, the child module's default AWS provider is remapped to use the `usw2` configuration.

### Multiple Alias Example

For modules requiring multiple provider configurations (like cross-region connectivity):

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  alias  = "usw1"
  region = "us-west-1"
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "usw2"
  region = "us-west-2"
}

module "tunnel" {
  source    = "./tunnel"
  providers = {
    aws.src = aws.usw1
    aws.dst = aws.usw2
  }
}
```

The child module can now reference `aws.src` and `aws.dst` for its resources. The documentation notes that "Non-default provider configurations are never automatically inherited, so any module that works in this manner always requires a `providers` argument."

## Key Behaviors

When you specify a `providers` argument, "the child module only has access to the provider configurations you specify," effectively controlling which provider instances are available within that module's scope.

resource "google_compute_security_policy" "cloud_armor_policy" {
  name        = var.policy_name
  description = var.policy_description
}

resource "google_compute_security_policy_rule" "rules" {
  for_each        = var.rules
  security_policy = google_compute_security_policy.cloud_armor_policy.name
  priority        = tonumber(each.key)
  action          = each.value.action
  preview         = each.value.preview
  description     = each.value.description

  match {
    versioned_expr = lookup(each.value, "versioned_expr", null)

    dynamic "config" {
      for_each = (
        contains(keys(each.value), "versioned_expr") &&
        each.value.versioned_expr != "" &&
        contains(keys(each.value), "src_ip_ranges") &&
        try(length(each.value.src_ip_ranges), 0) > 0
      ) ? [1] : []
      content {
        src_ip_ranges = each.value.src_ip_ranges
      }
    }

    dynamic "expr" {
      for_each = each.value.expr != null && each.value.expr != "" ? [1] : []
      content {
        expression = each.value.expr
      }
    }
  }

  dynamic "rate_limit_options" {
    for_each = (
      can(each.value.rate_limit_options) &&
      contains(["rate_based_ban", "throttle", "fairshare"], lower(each.value.action))
    ) ? [1] : []
    content {
      conform_action = "allow"
      exceed_action  = "deny(429)"
      enforce_on_key = "IP"

      rate_limit_threshold {
        count        = each.value.rate_limit_options.rate_limit_threshold.count
        interval_sec = each.value.rate_limit_options.rate_limit_threshold.interval_sec
      }

      dynamic "ban_threshold" {
        for_each = try(each.value.rate_limit_options.ban_threshold != null, false) ? [1] : []
        content {
          count        = each.value.rate_limit_options.ban_threshold.count
          interval_sec = each.value.rate_limit_options.ban_threshold.interval_sec
        }
      }

      ban_duration_sec = try(each.value.rate_limit_options.ban_duration_sec, null)
    }
  }

  lifecycle {
    ignore_changes = [
      rate_limit_options
    ]
  }
}
